CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a four-column chart showing how different plane cuts through a double cone produce different curves, with cone drawings across the top row and the resulting curves below. Each cone is drawn identically in pale blue as two nappes meeting point to point, the upper rim a solid blue ellipse and the lower rim a dashed one, and a red curve marks where a cutting plane meets it. The columns are titled Diagonal Slice, Horizontal Slice, Deep Vertical Slice, and Vertical Slice. The first shows a red ellipse tilted across the upper nappe; the second a red ellipse lying level across the upper nappe; the third two red arcs, one curving up through the top nappe and one down through the bottom; the fourth a single red arc cutting downward through the lower nappe only. Below each cone the curve is named and redrawn on its own in red, free of the cone: Ellipse as a flattened oval, Circle as a round closed curve, Hyperbola as two opposing branches, Parabola as a single upward-opening arc. Keeping each slice above its isolated curve lets the angle of the cut be read directly against the shape it yields. The chart sorts the four nondegenerate conic sections by the orientation of the generating plane.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Double cone (two nappes) meeting apex-to-apex at the origin, drawn as a pale blue translucent surface with dark blue outline silhouette lines
  - Upper nappe circular rim drawn as a solid dark-blue ellipse (top cap outline)
  - Lower nappe circular rim drawn as a dashed dark-blue ellipse (bottom cap outline, hidden edge)
  - Two straight dark-blue slant/silhouette edges running from upper rim through the apex to the lower rim, forming the hourglass X
  - Cutting plane through the cone (translucent, tilt-adjustable; implied in the source, shown here as the generator of the red curve)
  - Red intersection curve drawn on the cone surface: tilted closed ellipse on the upper nappe (Diagonal Slice)
  - Red intersection curve: level closed circle across the upper nappe (Horizontal Slice)
  - Red intersection curve: two opposing arcs, one sweeping up through the top nappe and one down through the bottom nappe with arrowheads (Deep Vertical Slice / hyperbola)
  - Red intersection curve: single arc cutting downward through the lower nappe with arrowheads (Vertical Slice / parabola)
  - Column heading text labels above each cone: 'Diagonal Slice', 'Horizontal Slice', 'Deep Vertical Slice', 'Vertical Slice'
  - Curve name labels below each cone: 'Ellipse', 'Circle', 'Hyperbola', 'Parabola'
  - Isolated planar red curves drawn alone below each cone: flattened oval (ellipse), round circle, two opposing branches (hyperbola), single upward-opening arc (parabola)

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Double cone: apex at origin, axis along +Y (screen-up). Each nappe extends to |y| = 2.0 with rim radius 1.05 at the rim, i.e. radius r = 0.525·|y| — half-angle from the axis α ≈ 27.7°, so a cone slant line makes ≈ 62.3° with the horizontal. Upper nappe rim (y = +2) drawn as a SOLID dark-blue ellipse; lower nappe rim (y = −2) drawn as a DASHED dark-blue ellipse (hidden far edge), matching the source. Two straight silhouette edges run from the upper rim through the apex to the lower rim, forming the hourglass X exactly at the mid-height of the figure. Overall cone height 4.0 units, width 2.1 units — taller than wide, as in the source (≈180 px half-height vs ≈95 px rim radius). Cutting plane: a square patch ~3.2 × 3.2 units, passing through a point offset +0.9 along the axis for the ellipse/circle cases and offset ~0.6 horizontally off the axis for the parabola/hyperbola cases; it rotates about the screen-horizontal (X) axis by the slider tilt θ measured from horizontal. Classification: θ = 0° circle; 0 < θ < 62.3° ellipse; θ = 62.3° parabola (plane parallel to a slant line, cuts one nappe only); θ > 62.3° hyperbola (plane steep enough to meet both nappes). Red curve is drawn as the exact intersection of that plane with the cone x²+z² = (0.525 y)². Arrowheads on the open ends of the parabola and hyperbola arcs, as in the source. 'Curve alone' view shows the same intersection re-expressed in plane coordinates, centered, red, no cone. Colors: cone fill pale blue #CFE0F5 at ~0.55 opacity, cone outline / rims dark blue #1F4E9C (~2 px), intersection curve red #E23A2E (~2.5 px), cutting plane light gray-blue #AFC6E4 at ~0.25 opacity, labels black bold sans-serif — column title ('Diagonal Slice' etc.) above the cone, curve name ('Ellipse' etc.) below it.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "planeTilt",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "Tilting the cutting plane past the slant angle of the cone is exactly what converts a closed ellipse into an unbounded parabola and then into a two-branch hyperbola — the conic type is decided by one angle.",
    "label": "Cutting-plane tilt from horizontal (°)",
    "range": [
      0,
      90,
      1
    ],
    "default": 30
  },
  {
    "id": "viewMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "The red curve on the 3D cone and the named 2D graph are the same object, seen once in space and once flattened into its own plane.",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "Cone slice",
      "Curve alone"
    ],
    "default": "Cone slice"
  },
  {
    "id": "conicStep",
    "type": "state",
    "teaches": "The four nondegenerate conics are classified by slice direction: diagonal, horizontal, deep vertical, and vertical cuts of the same double cone.",
    "label": "Conic column",
    "states": [
      "Ellipse (Diagonal Slice)",
      "Circle (Horizontal Slice)",
      "Hyperbola (Deep Vertical Slice)",
      "Parabola (Vertical Slice)"
    ],
    "default": "Ellipse (Diagonal Slice)"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitCone",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Rotating the double cone reveals that the red curve really lies on the cone surface in 3D and that its apparent shape in the textbook view is one projection of it."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Diagonal Slice → Ellipse",
    "narration": "A plane tilted about 30° from horizontal cuts clean through one nappe; the red intersection closes up into a tilted ellipse.",
    "state": {
      "conicStep": "Ellipse (Diagonal Slice)",
      "planeTilt": 30,
      "viewMode": "Cone slice"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "The ellipse alone",
    "narration": "Lift the curve off the cone: viewed inside its own plane it is the flattened oval labelled Ellipse.",
    "state": {
      "conicStep": "Ellipse (Diagonal Slice)",
      "planeTilt": 30,
      "viewMode": "Curve alone"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Horizontal Slice → Circle",
    "narration": "Flatten the plane to 0°, perpendicular to the cone's axis, and the ellipse tightens into a perfect circle — the special case of zero tilt.",
    "state": {
      "conicStep": "Circle (Horizontal Slice)",
      "planeTilt": 0,
      "viewMode": "Cone slice"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Vertical Slice → Parabola",
    "narration": "Tilt to about 62°, exactly parallel to a slant line of the cone. The curve can no longer close; it escapes down the single lower nappe as a parabola.",
    "state": {
      "conicStep": "Parabola (Vertical Slice)",
      "planeTilt": 62,
      "viewMode": "Cone slice"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Deep Vertical Slice → Hyperbola",
    "narration": "Past the slant angle, at 85°, the plane is steep enough to strike both nappes at once, producing the two opposing red branches of a hyperbola.",
    "state": {
      "conicStep": "Hyperbola (Deep Vertical Slice)",
      "planeTilt": 85,
      "viewMode": "Cone slice"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Both branches alone",
    "narration": "Isolated in its plane, the same intersection is the familiar two-branch hyperbola graph shown in the bottom row.",
    "state": {
      "conicStep": "Hyperbola (Deep Vertical Slice)",
      "planeTilt": 85,
      "viewMode": "Curve alone"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.7
}

NOTES: Build the cone as two ConeGeometry/LatheGeometry nappes with a MeshBasicMaterial (flat pale-blue fill, DoubleSide, transparent, depthWrite false) plus separate LineSegments for the rims and silhouette edges so the dashed lower rim uses LineDashedMaterial (call computeLineDistances). Compute the red conic analytically each frame rather than by mesh boolean: parameterize the cutting plane by its in-plane basis (u, v), substitute into x²+z² = k²y², and solve the resulting quadratic for the intersection — emit one closed Line for ellipse/circle, one open curve for parabola, two Lines for the hyperbola branches. Clamp the parameter range so open branches terminate at the rim planes y = ±2, then attach small cone-mesh arrowheads at those ends. Render the red curve slightly offset along the surface normal (or with polygonOffset / depthTest false at low opacity for the hidden half) so it reads through the translucent cone like the source's solid red line. Use an orthographic-style axonometric camera (OrthographicCamera or a long-lens PerspectiveCamera) so the top and bottom rims are congruent ellipses with no perspective taper. Labels via CSS2DRenderer sprites pinned above and below the cone. The 'Curve alone' toggle should smoothly rotate the camera into the cutting plane's normal while fading out the cone, so the learner sees the 3D curve flatten into the textbook 2D graph rather than being swapped abruptly.

Follow the interaction plan above.

